  "post": {
    "caption": "A wedding gift began sweetheart brooches. On their wedding day in 1886, the Earl of Airlie gave his wife a brooch copying his regiment's badge, and she said other army wives copied her. They spread in the Boer War and boomed in World War One. They were small copies of regimental cap badges, and hundreds of designs were made, mostly in London and Birmingham. Soldiers bought them at jewelry shops and army camps. Despite the name, they also went to mothers and sisters, and women wore them with pride and as good luck charms. Museums from London to Canberra hold them today.",
